Recount term statistics a page at a time, without holding the metapage

Applies to vchord_bm25 0.3.0 (upstream tag 0.3.0, commit c610b7ab), after 0001
to 0003.

After a VACUUM that deleted documents, amvacuumcleanup (vacuum.rs) recounted
the number of live documents that hold each term. It held the metapage for
reading while it counted, so inserts waited, and then for writing while it
stored the counts, so searches waited too. Both steps visited every term id
below term_id_cnt, which is the largest term id indexed plus one, not the
number of terms. Storing a count cost a page write and a WAL record per term
id, whether or not the count changed. The counts were held in one vector of
term_id_cnt entries, outside maintenance_work_mem. Neither loop checked for
interrupts, and the metapage lock held them off anyway, so the step could not
be cancelled. The metapage was released and taken again between the two
steps, so an insert that ran in between lost its counts.

The recount now takes the lock an insert takes to seal the growing segment.
An insert that cannot get that lock skips sealing, so inserts go on, and the
sealed segment and the growing segment's pages stay where the recount reads
them. It then works through one term statistic page (2,040 term ids) at a
time:
- It counts the live documents of the page's sealed terms from the term
  information page and the posting lists, with no lock held. It copies the
  delete bitmap once; bulk delete is over, so no bit changes.
- It holds the metapage for reading only while it counts the documents
  inserted since the last page and reads the page's stored counts. An insert
  adds to the counts only while it holds the metapage for writing, so the two
  match exactly.
- It then writes the page, if a count on it changed, under one WAL record,
  moving each count by the difference it measured. An insert that ran after
  the metapage was released keeps its addition.
- It holds no buffer lock from one page to the next, and applies the
  cost-based delay and checks for interrupts between pages. The seal lock
  stays held throughout, so no insert seals the growing segment until the
  recount ends.

A recount that stops part way leaves the statistics it has not reached still
counting deleted documents. Upstream recounted only when the same VACUUM had
removed documents, so the next VACUUM repaired them only if it removed some
too; one whose bulk delete found every document already marked, after a cancel
or a crash, never did. The bulk delete now notes, in the WAL record that takes
documents off the counts, that a recount is owed, and only a recount that
reaches its last page clears the note. amvacuumcleanup recounts whenever the
note is set, so the next VACUUM that cleans up the index, including one that
removes nothing, finishes what an interrupted one owed. A VACUUM run with
INDEX_CLEANUP OFF, or stopped by the wraparound failsafe, skips the cleanup and
leaves the note for the one after. ANALYZE never recounts. When that VACUUM
removed nothing, no bulk delete counted the index's tuples, so the cleanup
reports the metapage's document count instead of leaving pg_class.reltuples at
0. The note is the high bit of the metapage's version word, which nothing
reads, and an index built before this patch reads as owing nothing.

Memory is the delete bitmap, the growing segment's terms and one page of
counts. No on-disk format or SQL changes.

Measured on 100,000 documents with 20,000 deleted and one term id of
728,984,817, so term_id_cnt was 728,984,818, unpatched then patched:
- the cleanup: 2,906 s, then 2.7 s (17.5 s under autovacuum's cost delay);
- the longest search: 2,633 s, then 0.002 s;
- the longest insert: 2,906 s, then 0.008 s;
- the WAL the VACUUM wrote: 35.4 GiB, then 0.3 MiB;
- the VACUUM backend's anonymous memory: 2.72 GiB, then 3.4 MiB.

Also measured:
- A cancel during the cleanup, at about 3,000,000 term ids: unpatched, VACUUM
  stopped after 10.8 s; patched, after 0.001 s.
- Inserts running through the VACUUM, 50,000 documents over about 3,050,000
  term ids: unpatched, 275 inserts got through and 15 term statistics were wrong
  afterwards; patched, 8,672 got through and none was wrong. With a 2 ms pause
  forced between reading a page and writing it, writing the new count instead
  of the difference left 9 and 11 wrong in two runs; the difference, none.
- 200,000 documents of 120 terms each over the same 728,984,818 term ids,
  40,000 deleted, under autovacuum's cost delay: the cleanup took 162 s, no
  search waited more than 0.07 s, and the WAL was 2.75 GiB, a full-page image
  of each changed statistic page.
- 100,000 documents, 40,000 deleted, the VACUUM backend killed during the
  recount, then a VACUUM with nothing to remove: recounting only when that
  VACUUM removed documents, as upstream did, left all 42,001 affected
  statistics too high; recounting what is owed left none. A cancel between the
  bulk delete and the cleanup, followed by a cancel during the recount, gave
  the same.

